# Thermally Crosslinked Hole Conductor Enables Stable Inverted Perovskite Solar Cells with 23.9% Efficiency

## abstract
Poly[bis(4-phenyl)(2,4,6-trimethylphenyl)amine] (PTAA) represents the state-of-the-art hole transport material (HTM) in inverted perovskite solar cells (PSCs). However, unsatisfied surface properties of PTAA and high energy disorder in the bulk film hinder the further enhancement of device performance. Herein, a simple small molecule 10-(4-(3,6-dimethoxy-9H-carbazol-9-yl)phenyl)-3,7-bis(4-vinylphenyl)-10H-phenoxazine (MCz-VPOZ) is strategically developed for in situ fabrication of polymer hole conductor (CL-MCz) via a facile and low-temperature cross-linking technology. The resulting polymer CL-MCz offers high energy ordering and improved electrical conductivity, as well as appropriate energy-level alignment, enabling efficient charge carrier collection in the devices. Meanwhile, CL-MCz synchronously provides satisfied surface wettability and interfacial functionalization, facilitating the formation of high-quality perovskite films with fewer bulk iodine vacancies and suppressed carrier recombination. Significantly, the device with CL-MCz yields a champion efficiency of 23.9% along with an extremely low energy loss down to 0.41 eV, which represents the highest reported efficiency for non-PTAA-based polymer HTMs in inverted PSCs. Furthermore, the corresponding unencapsulated devices exhibit competitive shelf-life stability under various operational stressors up to 2500 h, reflecting high promises of CL-MCz in the scalable PSC application. This work underscores the promising potential of the cross-linking approach in preparing low-cost, stable, and efficient polymer HTMs toward reliable PSCs.

Poly[bis(4-phenyl)(2,4,6-trimethylphenyl)amine] (PTAA) represents the state-ofthe-art hole transport material (HTM) in inverted perovskite solar cells (PSCs). However, unsatisfied surface properties of PTAA and high energy disorder in the bulk film hinder the further enhancement of device performance. Herein, a simple small molecule 10-(4-(3,6-dimethoxy-9H-carbazol-9-yl)phenyl)-3,7-bis(4- vinylphenyl)-10H-phenoxazine (MCz-VPOZ) is strategically developed for in situ fabrication of polymer hole conductor (CL-MCz) via a facile and low-temperature cross-linking technology. The resulting polymer CL-MCz offers high energy ordering and improved electrical conductivity, as well as appropriate energylevel alignment, enabling efficient charge carrier collection in the devices. Meanwhile, CL-MCz synchronously provides satisfied surface wettability and interfacial functionalization, facilitating the formation of high-quality perovskite films with fewer bulk iodine vacancies and suppressed carrier recombination. Significantly, the device with CL-MCz yields a champion efficiency of $23.9\%$ along with an extremely low energy loss down to 0.41 eV, which represents the highest reported efficiency for non-PTAA-based polymer HTMs in inverted PSCs. Furthermore, the corresponding unencapsulated devices exhibit competitive shelf-life stability under various operational stressors up to $2500\textmd h$ , reflecting high promises of CL-MCz in the scalable PSC application. This work underscores the promising potential of the cross-linking approach in preparing low-cost, stable, and efficient polymer HTMs toward reliable PSCs.
## 1. Introduction
Inverted p-i-n perovskite solar cells (PSCs) with perovskite film deposited on the hole transport layer (HTL) offer the advantages of simple fabrication process, lowtemperature preparation, and reliable operational stability, showing high compatibility with flexible and tandem devices.[1–3] However, the power conversion efficiency (PCE) of inverted devices has rarely exceeded $23\%$ due to high energy losses which is mainly limited by the intrinsic optoelectronic properties of HTL as well as inferior interfaces with perovskite.[4–7] Specifically, HTL acting as a crystallization substrate of perovskite film plays a decisive role in regulating perovskite defects chemistry and charge carrier dynamics at the buried interfaces.[8–10] Therefore, asides from the conventional requirements, such as high mobility and aligned energy levels for promoting the charge extraction and transport,[11–13] superior surface properties (e.g., film morphology, surface wettability) of the HTL is essential to regulate perovskite crystallization and form high-quality perovskite films.[14] Poly[bis(4- phenyl)(2,4,6-trimethylphenyl)amine] (PTAA) represents the most used and efficient HTL in inverted PSCs, showing the advantages of simple fabrication, high transparency, mechanical flexibility, and good stability.[6] However, the unmatched energy levels and low carrier mobility of PTAA limit the carrier extraction from the perovskite layer.[9] In addition, PTAA with long main chains exhibits a high degree of conformational freedom leading to high energy disorder.[15,16] The high energy disorder could broaden the band tail electronic states and thus narrow the splitting of quasi-Fermi levels,[16–18] which thus increases the carrier transport barrier. Moreover, high hydrophobicity and poor interfacial contact for PTAA often require additional surface post-treatment or interfacial functionalization to reduce the defects at the buried interface.[19,20] Meanwhile, the tedious synthetic process and poor batch-to-batch repeatability of polymers hinder their scalable application. In addressing these issues, attempts to engineer the HTL through chemical doping,[21,22] surface treatment,[23] structural modification,[24–27] interfacial passivation,[28–30] and multilayer strategy,[31] have been successfully explored, notably improving the device performance. Unfortunately, the device performance is still lagging behind the PSCs with conventional n-i-p structure, which implies clear needs to develop alternative HTLs to further enhance the performance of inverted PSCs.
Here, we demonstrate a facile and low-temperature crosslinking strategy to in situ fabricate polymer HTL based on a carbazole molecule 10-(4-(3,6-dimethoxy-9H-carbazol-9-yl) phenyl)-3,7-bis(4-vinyphenyl)-10H-phenoxazine  (MCz-VPOZ) for inverted PSCs. This spontaneous, straightforward deposition of polymer HTL from a precursor solution avoids tedious synthesis and poor batch-to-batch reproducibility. Meanwhile, the polymer CL-MCz with superior structural planarity and high energy ordering exhibits over 25  times enhancement of carrier mobility than PTAA, leading to notable improvement of charge-collection efficiency in devices. We show that the HTL based on CL-MCz endowing remarkable chemical resistivity and superior surface wettability enables effective modification of perovskite buried interfaces, resulting in the formation of homogeneous perovskite film and the minimization of microstructural defects at the perovskite interfaces. Besides, CL-MCz with multifunctional groups can sufficiently passivate the ionic defects in perovskite, favoring increasing the carrier lifetime and reducing the recombination losses. These improvements lead to a simultaneous increase of all photovoltaic parameters, resulting in a champion PCE of $23.9\%$ with an extremely small voltage loss of only $0.41~\mathrm{V},$ which represents the highest reported efficiency for non-PTAA polymer HTLs in inverted PSCs. More encouragingly, the unsealed devices based on CL-MCz demonstrates remarkably enhanced stability under various environmental conditions for up to $2500\mathrm{h}$ , which guarantees its high reliability in future scalable application.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Material Design, Synthesis, and Characterization
To elucidate the defect passivation effects of MCz-VPOZ, first-principles calculations were carried out using density functional theory (DFT) for gaining theoretical insight into the interaction between the HTL and perovskite surface. We applied a recently-proposed Bayesian optimization structure search (BOSS) method to evaluate the adsorption geometry and defect formation energies between the HTL and perovskites.[38] For more efficient modeling, we used a partition strategy with two smaller molecules, 9-phenylcarbazole (PC) and 10-phenylphenoxazine (PPO), to represent the most functional parts of MCz-VPOZ, as sketched in Figure 1B. Based on the BOSS-DFT results, the most stable adsorption geometry of MCz-VPOZ on $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ can be determined by minimizing the sum of formation energies of both systems (e.g., $\mathrm{PC}/\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ $(-0.620$ eV) and $\mathrm{PPO/MAPbI}_{3}$ $(-0.918\ \mathrm{eV})\$ , with a constrain that the adsorbed PC and PPO molecules can adaptively form an MCzVPOZ molecule based on the specific adsorption locations. As a reference, the adsorption energies of both 2,4,6-trimethylN,N-diphenylaniline (DPA) and N-phenyl-N-(4-vinylphenyl)- 2-naphthalenamine (PVN) (DPA, PVN represent the monomer analog of PTAA and VNPB, respectively, as shown in Figure S4, Supporting Information) at $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ are less negative $(-0.614$ and $-0.465\ \mathrm{~eV},$ respectively). This is presumably due to that both PC and PPO possess a relatively large $\pi$ conjugate system which favors a strong electronic interaction with the perovskite surface. In contrast, both DPA and PVN display much more twisted geometries, so that a strong interaction between the perovskite surface and the quasi-parallel large $\pi$ system cannot be established (Figure 1C). DFT-calculated total-electrondensity difference (Figure 1D) intuitively rationalizes the strong interaction between MCz-VPOZ and $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ , for example, the transferred charge density between either PC or PPO and the $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ substrate is distributed over the whole interface, while the electronic interaction between either DPA or PVN and $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ exhibits an obvious localized character. In addition, we have also calculated the formation energy of the surface iodinevacancy defect (Figure  1E). The values (at the I-rich limit) for PC- and $\mathrm{PPO-MAPbI}_{3}$ are 2.793 and 2.801 eV, respectively, which are obviously higher than the value for the bare $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ $(2.645\ \mathrm{eV})$ as well as that for DPA and $\mathrm{PVN-MAPbI}_{3}$ (2.659 and $2.650\mathrm{eV},$ respectively). These results further indicate that $\mathrm{{MCz}}$ - VPOZ could provide more efficient passivation effects to perovskite than PTAA and VNPB.
We applied a facile, low-temperature, thermal cross-linking strategy to in situ fabricate polymer thin film on a conductive glass substrate, as illustrated in Figure S5 (Supporting Information). The spontaneous self-assembly process enables the formation of a polymer network structure, which favors sufficient charge transportation.[35] As demonstrated in the Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (Figure S6A, Supporting Information), the gradual decrease of the peak intensity at $1654~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{=}\mathsf{C})$ under the annealing process indicates the occurrence of thermal crosslinking of MCz-VPOZ. By roughly analyzing the peak-area ratio, an estimated crosslinking degree of $76.9\%$ was obtained after thermal annealing. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was further carried out to examine the structural variation of the MCz-VPOZ film before and after the cross-linking process (Figure S6B, Supporting Information). The ratio of $s p^{2}\mathsf{C}\mathrm{=C}$ double bonds to $s p^{3}\mathsf{C-C}$ single bonds in the film decreased from 5.4 to 1.9 when a temperature of $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was applied, confirming the formation of polymer CL-MCz after thermal treatment. The chemical resistance of CL-MCz film was further evaluated by measuring the change of UV–vis absorption after solvent washing by dimethylformamide (DMF), as shown in Figure S7 (Supporting Information). Notably, the film of CL-MCz and CL-VNPB prepared at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ exhibited remarkable solvent resistance to DMF compared to those made with a low crosslinking temperature (e. g. $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ), which guarantees the high anti-solvent resistance of the films during the solution process of perovskite deposition.
To further probe the chemical interaction between CL-MCz and perovskite, we evaluated the Pb 4f orbitals in XPS (Figure S8, Supporting Information). The typical two peaks associated with the spin-orbit splitting in the high-resolution Pb 4f corelevel spectra were shifted to lower binding energies, indicating that CL-MCz could interact with uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions,[26,39] which is likely due to the increased electron cloud density of $\mathrm{Pb}$ after electron donation from the polymer. Moreover, the crosslinking of molecules favors substantial enhancement in film conductivity, that is, ${\approx}1.5$ times higher conductivity was obtained for CL-MCz than that of the precursor MCz-VPOZ (Figure S9, Supporting Information, Table 1), which may indicate the cross-linking process favors the formation of organized film structures. Ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) results (Figure S10, Supporting Information) demonstrate CL-MCz film has a superior energy alignment which favors improving the carrier collection efficiency.
The optoelectronic properties of three representative HTLs were thoroughly investigated and compared. The absorption spectra of HTMs were presented in Figure S11 (Supporting Information) and Table  1, showing that the absorption edge of CL-MCz was red-shifted compared to those of PTAA and CL-VNPB. As shown in Figure S12 (Supporting Information), the light transmittance of $\mathrm{CL}{\cdot}\mathrm{MCz}$ is slightly higher than that of PTAA and CL-VNPB in the range of $340{\mathrm{-}}400\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which could favor enhancing the light harvesting by the perovskite.[20] Thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA) of MCz-VPOZ has been performed to examine the thermal properties (Figure S13, Supporting Information). The monomer MCz-VPOZ could have been mostly polymerized during the TGA measurement considering its low crosslinking temperature of $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , so that the high degradation temperature $\left(T_{\mathrm{d}}\right)$ up to $465~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ could demonstrate high thermal stability of the formed polymer CL-MCz.
Moreover, the degree of energy ordering (DEO) in HTLs plays crucial roles in the hole mobility, density of charge carriers, and energy losses in the devices.[16] Considering the important role of temperature on charge hopping behavior in semiconducting materials, the temperature dependence of polymer mobility was investigated in order to evaluate the DEO of these materials.[40] In the space-charge-limited holeonly devices with a structure of ITO/HTMs/Au, PTAA presented a significant enhancement (over 100  times) of carrier mobility upon further increasing the temperature from 195 to 275  K. Contrary to this, a much smaller response of carrier mobility to temperature was observed for CL-MCz (1.44 times) and CL-VNPB (1.45 times) (Figure 2A–C). Given that organic semiconductors with low DEO usually exhibit strong temperature dependence due to high activation energies, the results indicate cross-linked polymer CL-MCz generates high energy ordering in thin films, which would be beneficial for charge conduction and film reliability. According to the non-Arrhenius-type temperature dependence (as shown in
Equation (1)), the Gaussian density of states (DOS) width $\sigma$ is calculated as a parameter for quantifying energy disorder:[41,42]
$$
\mu(T){=}{\mu_{0}\exp}\Biggl(-\Biggl(\frac{\sigma}{k T}\Biggr)^{2}\Biggr)
$$
where $\mu_{0}$ is the pre-factor, $k$ is the Boltzmann constant, and $T$ is the temperature, respectively. As shown in Figure  2D, the Gaussian DOS width $\sigma$ of the cross-linked polymer CL-MCz $(0.039\mathrm{eV})$ is greatly reduced compared to that of PTAA $(0.107\mathrm{eV})$ , which is mostly associated with more ordered molecular packing in the film. According to the brief estimation by the Mott–Gurney law (Figure S14, Supporting Information), CL-MCz exhibited a hole mobility of $5.2\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{V}^{-1}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1},$ which is about twice that of CL-VNPB $(2.2\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{V}^{-1}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1})$ and one order of magnitude higher than that of PTAA $(2.03\ \times\ 10^{-5}\ \mathrm{\cm^{2}}\ \mathrm{\V^{-1}}\ \mathrm{\bar{\s}^{-1}})$ . Moreover, compared to PTAA $(4.47\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}})$ , CL-MCz demonstrated an order of magnitude higher conductivity of $4.67\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ , in accordance with the high DEO in cross-linked thin film (Figures S9,S15, Supporting Information).
Kelvin probe force microscopy (KPFM) and UPS measurements were employed to probe the surface energetic variation of these HTMs.[43] A much larger contact potential difference (CPD) was observed for CL-MCz $(729~\mathrm{mV})$ , as compared to PTAA $(385~\mathrm{mV})$ and CL-VNPB ( $(614~\mathrm{mV})$ ) (Figure  2E), which indicates CL-MCz has a relatively higher work function (WF).[26] Besides, the high uniformity of potential distribution for CL-MCz may reflect high DEO and high film homogeneity. In addition, the WF and valence band maximum (VBM) of this can be estimated by UPS (Figure 2F,G). The WF of CL-MCz is shifted ${\approx}320~\mathrm{meV}$ toward the VBM compared to that of PTAA, reflecting that the surface of CL-MCz is more p-type against charge accumulation at the interface. The WF offset of CL-MCz is $30\mathrm{\meV}$ higher than that of CL-VNPB, proving high feasibility for charge extraction and transport. As depicted in Figure 2H, CL-MCz showed a deeper HOMO level $(-5.28~\mathrm{eV})$ than that of PTAA $(-5.07\ \mathrm{eV})$ , which could be beneficial for charge extraction and obtaining a high open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ . Notably, a lower DEO favors the formation of broadened localized DOS, where nonequilibrium carriers are liable to relax down in gap tail states of the DOS, limiting the density of photogenerated holes in the DOS tails and causing a high voltage loss. As illustrated in Figure 2H, the crosslinked CL-MCz film with high DEO favors generating a narrower tail states width, leading to suppression of the non-radiative recombination  (NRR)  and thus substantial increase of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ in the devices.
## 2.2. Microstructures and Carrier Dynamics at the Interfaces
The surface nature of HTLs represents another determining factor influencing the perovskite crystallization and interfacial properties. Therefore, the contact angles (CA) of these three HTLs were probed to evaluate their surface wettability (Figure S16, Supporting Information). For both water and perovskite solutions, the CL-MCz film (e. g. $77.3^{\circ}$ for water) showed higher hydrophilicity compared to that of PTAA $(94.1^{\circ}$ for water), which favors obtaining the full-coverage perovskite film. As measured by atomic force microscopy (AFM), CL-MCz shows a higher roughness compared to PTAA (Figure S17, Supporting Information). A smaller CA and high roughness for the hydrophilic surface $(\mathrm{C}\ <90^{\circ})$ ) of CL-MCz film favors increasing the surface tension and obtaining a higher wettability.
To determine whether the HTL substrate influences the crystallization and surface morphology of the perovskite films, a film lift-off process was applied to exfoliate the buried interface for direct visualization (Figure 3A), details of which are summarized in the Experimental Section. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) measurements were employed to study the morphologies of both tops and buried surfaces of perovskite films, as shown in Figure  3B–D and Figure S18 (Supporting Information). The grain size of perovskite formed on top of the CL-MCz $(\approx500-600\ \mathrm{nm})$ is much larger than that grown on PTAA $(\approx300-400~\mathrm{nm})$ . Besides, the perovskite grains based on CL-MCz are uniaxial and vertically oriented, which is beneficial for reducing the grain boundary density and thus decreasing carrier recombination sites. In addition, compared to the blurred and nonuniform grains at the buried interfaces between perovskite and PTAA, the perovskite film obtained on $\mathrm{CL}{\cdot}\mathrm{MCz}$ is composed of large grains with uniform size distribution as well as high crystallinity, evident by the XRD spectra (Figure S19, Supporting Information). To extract the defect density in the film, space-charge limited current (SCLC) was measured on a hole-only device with a structure of $\mathrm{ITO/HTL}/$ perovskite/spiro-OMeTAD/Ag (Figure S20, Supporting Information).[44] The trap-filled limited voltage $(V_{\mathrm{TFL}})$ of the device was decreased to $0.63\mathrm{~V~}$ when using CL-MCz to replace the PTAA $(V_{\mathrm{TFL}}=0.87$ V). Besides, the estimated trap defect density $(N_{t})$ of $4.70\times10^{-15}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ is $\approx30\%$ lower than that of PTAA of $6.52\times10^{-15}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ . The inhibition of interfacial defects can also be verified by XPS (Figure S21, Supporting Information). The typical peaks of Pb $4f$ in ITO/perovskite/CL-MCz samples were shifted toward lower binding energy compared to PTAA, indicating that CL-MCz has a stronger passivation to the perovskite layer originating from the interaction between the methoxy group containing lone pair electrons and uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ of perovskite.[29] This strong interaction may provide nucleation sites for perovskite to promote perovskite crystallization, which can be reflected by high-quality perovskite films with larger grain size and more uniform size distribution, as demonstrated in AFM and KPFM characterization (Figure  3E–G). Benefitted from the conformal-coating of $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ by thermal evaporation, a higher root mean square (RMS) roughness of perovskite film deposited on CL-MCz could increase the contact area with $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ and facilitate the electron collection at perovskite/electron transport layer (ETL) interface (Figure 3H). In addition, the perovskite film on the CL-MCz showed a more uniform CPD compared to the other two samples (Figure  3I), which would narrow the interfacial electronic states when in contact with $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ , leading to reduced interfacial recombination, as similarly observed in a recent study.[45]
To understand the influence of HTLs on carrier dynamics, photoluminescence (PL) was measured on the device of ITO/ HTL/perovskite. The devices based on CL-MCz exhibited a much lower PL intensity compared to PTAA and CL-VNPB, meaning more efficient hole extraction at perovskite/HTL interfaces. This was further confirmed by time-resolved PL which presented a shorter fluorescence quenching lifetime of 51.7 ns for CL-MCz-based devices than PTAA (162.4 ns), indicating faster hole extraction (Figure 4A,B, Table S3, Supporting Information). The long PL lifetime for PTAA implies that the hole extraction rate could be inhibited by high energy disorder in PTAA film and mismatched energy levels. Moreover, the carrier dynamics of the bare perovskite film exfoliated from the HTL substrate were also studied (Figure  4C,D, Table S4, Supporting Information). It has to mention that after exfoliation the polymer HTL has mostly remained on the ITO glass substrate due to strong adhesion, as proved in our previous work.[46] Compared to the PTAA-based sample $\tau_{\mathrm{ave}}=315.9~\mathrm{ns}_{,}$ ), the average carrier lifetime $(\tau_{\mathrm{ave}})$ in CL-MCz-based devices is over threefold enhancement $\prime\tau_{\mathrm{ave}}=1071.7\mathrm{ns}$ ). The enhanced PL intensity for the perovskite film exfoliated from CL-MCz mostly results from reduced NRR and higher crystallinity of perovskite film, all of which are beneficial for reducing $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ losses and device performance.
The evidence of the enhanced carrier lifetime can also be provided by transient reflection spectroscopy (TR). For both top and the buried surface of perovskite films, the relevant bleach peaks appear right side after photoexcitation (Figure  4E–G; Figure S22, Supporting Information). As shown in Figure S23 (Supporting Information), the antisymmetric signal ${\approx}750\ \mathrm{nm}$ in the TR spectrum origins from exciton bleaching, which is caused by the phase space-filling effect of photocarriers.[47] The thin-film interference of the probe light mainly exists in the near- and sub-bandgap region, that is, the optically thin region, accounting for the oscillation signal in the range from 750 to $850\ \mathrm{nm}$ .[48,49] The interference magnitude is related to the variation of the complex refractive index after optical excitation. As the probing wavelength decreases, the interference fades away and the surface reflection signal remains. Thus, we select the TR kinetics at wavelengths shorter than $700\ \mathrm{nm}$ to track the surface carrier dynamics that are mainly governed by carrier diffusion and surface carrier recombination,[50] both of which can lead to surface depopulation in the detection region. According to the 1D diffusion equation, the diffusion rate is determined by the density gradient of photocarriers at the instant of excitation. The same pump wavelength (e.g., $550\mathrm{nm}$ ) was utilized for the three samples to ensure the same initial photocarrier density gradient. Under such conditions, the diversity of TR kinetics reflects the variation of the surface recombination rate. From the kinetic comparison (Figure 4H), the surface recombination rate for the top surface of the perovskite films treated with CL-MCz (1843.5 ps) is sixfold and ninefold longer than the CL-VNPB (301.4 ps) sample and the PTAA sample (198.5 ps), indicating that the high-quality perovskite crystallization promotes the carrier transport. Although the perovskite bottom interfaces exhibit high uniformity and low void defects for CL-MCz (Figure 3D), the carrier decay kinetics for the perovskite buried interfaces followed the order of PTAA (934.5 ps) $>$ CL-VNPB (174.7 ps) $>$ CL-MCz (105.1 ps) (Figure 4I). Considering the high surface sensitivity of TR measurement, the faster PL decay for CL-MCz could indicate that some CL-MCz residual induces rapid quenching and sufficient extraction of charge carriers from the HTL/perovskite interface. Additionally, the estimated carrier diffusion length in the perovskite film based on CL-MCz $(62.77\upmu\mathrm{m})$ is ${\approx}10$ and 20 times longer than CL-VNPB and PTAA, indicating that the NRR in the CL-MCz-based devices has been effectively inhibited (Figure 4J).
## 2.3. Photovoltaic Performance and Device Stability
Based on these HTLs, we fabricated inverted p-i-n devices with a configuration of ITO/HTL/perovskite $\mathrm{/C_{60}/B C P/A g}$ , where $(\mathrm{FA_{0.17}M A_{0.94}P b I_{3.11}})_{0.95}(\mathrm{PbCl_{2}})_{0.05}$ with a bandgap of $1.58\:\mathrm{eV}$ was selected as a light absorbing layer.[26] We found that the CL-MCz annealed at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ gave the best device efficiency compared to the monomer $\mathrm{{MCz}}$ -VPOZ and the polymer CL-MCz synthesized at higher temperatures (e. g., $210~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ ) (Figure S24, Supporting Information). Moreover, the effect of different concentrations $(0.5{-}2~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1})$ of MCz-VPOZ on device performance was presented in Figure S25 (Supporting Information), showing notable PCE reduction as concentration increases, which could be attributed to the higher film resistivity due to thicker HTL, lower film uniformity (Figure S26, Supporting Information) and higher defect concentration in devices (Figure S27, Supporting Information), as examined by SEM and SCLC measurements.
The current–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves of devices in both forward and reverse scan were measured under AM $1.5\mathrm{~G~}$ simulated solar illumination at $100\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}$ (Figure 5A–C), and the detailed device parameters including hysteresis index are listed in Table 2. The control device based on PTAA showed the highest PCE of $20.9\%$ under forward scan with a $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of 1.117 V, $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $22.63\mathrm{\mA\cm^{.2}}$ , FF of $82.87\%$ . The statistics of PCE and detailed parameters are exhibited in Figure  5D and Figure
Meanwhile, the perovskite defect passivation makes the surface more n-type, which promotes charge extraction at the perovskite/ETL interface so as to inhibit charge accumulation and reduce carrier recombination loss.[51] The carrier recombination and extraction dynamic of the complete device were further explored for understanding the insights into the improvement of device performance. According to the trapassisted Shockley−Read−Hall recombination mechanism, the dependence of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ on light intensities was measured for exploring nonradiative recombination dynamics. As shown in Figure  5F, a much smaller ideal factor $(\mathrm{n}=1.12~k_{\mathrm{B}}T~\mathrm{q}^{-1})$ was obtained for CL-MCz-based devices compared to those with PTAA $(\mathrm{n}=1.42\ k_{\mathrm{B}}T\ \mathrm{q}^{-1})$ and CL-VNPB $(\mathrm{n}=1.37~k_{\mathrm{B}}T~\mathrm{q}^{-1})$ , indicating that the NRR is significantly inhibited in CL-MCz-based devices due to the high-quality perovskite film and good interfacial contact. The transient photovoltage response for the completed devices was also measured to examine the nonradiative carrier dynamics. CL-MCz-based devices exhibited the longest charge recombination lifetime of $100.67~{\upmu\mathrm{s}}$ compared to the other samples (PTAA: $33.08~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ , and CL-VNPB: $37.85~\upmu\mathrm{s}^{\prime}$ ), which further validates the reduction of defects-assisted NRR losses in the case of CL-MCz, as illustrated in Figure S32 A and Table S7 (Supporting Information).
To examine the carrier extraction ability of these transport materials, the photocurrent density $(J_{\mathrm{ph}})$ versus effective applied voltage $(\mathrm{V_{eff}})$ characterization was performed (Figure  5G).[52] The charge collection efficiency $\left({\mathrm{P_{C}}}\right)$ can be calculated using the equation $\mathrm{P_{C}}=J_{\mathrm{ph}}/J_{\mathrm{sat}},$ quantifying the performance of charge extraction and transport in the device, where the $J_{\mathrm{sat}}$ is the saturation photocurrent density.[53] The device based on CL-MCz showed a much higher Pc (0.986, 0.879) than PTAA (0.867, 0.719) and CL-VNPB (0.925, 0.775) under both short-circuit and MPP conditions, respectively. This result further indicates that the high mobility and low energy disorder in crosslinked CL-MCz promote the carrier extraction and collection, which can be further confirmed by faster transient photocurrent response to light excitation, as shown in Figure $\mathrm{~S32~B~}$ and Table S8 (Supporting Information). In addition, the influence of carrier dynamics on the device performance was investigated via the Mott-Schottky and electrical impedance spectroscopy (EIS). According to the plots of $1/\mathsf{C}^{2}$ versus voltage (Figure  5H), the built-in potential $(V_{\mathrm{bi}})$ of devices based on CL-MCz (0.923 V) was much higher than PTAA (0.835 V) and CL-VNPB (0.918 V), as summarized in Table S9 (Supporting Information). A higher $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ for CL-MCz favors to provide a stronger driving force for the carrier separation at the HTL/ perovskite interface, which explains the enhancement of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ in devices. Moreover, CL-MCz-based devices exhibited lower carrier density $(2.0\times10^{18}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3})$ at interfaces than PTAA $(3.5\times10^{18}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3})$ , indicating severe charge accumulation occurred in PTAA-based devices, which is probably attributed to the low hole mobility and mismatched HOMO levels. Furthermore, as estimated by
EIS (Figure S33, Table S10, Supporting Information),[51] the reduced contact resistance $(R_{\mathrm{co}})$ and higher recombination resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{rec}}\right)$ for the device based on CL-MCz corroborates the enhancement of charge extraction and inhibition of NRR, which is consistent with the aforementioned results.
Stability is another important parameter to evaluate the HTLs, we therefore first tested the reliability of the precursor solution (MCz-VPOZ and VNPB) during long-term storage, as illustrated in Figure S34 (Supporting Information). Clearly, the solution of VNPB became very cloudy after one week, which could be related to the aggregation-prone nature of the naphthalene core in VNPB. As a result, the CL-VNPB made by the aged precursor solution showed significantly degraded device performance. In contrast, MCz-VPOZ-based precursor demonstrated outstanding material stability and negligible performance variation in PSCs under the same condition. We also measured the photostability of the unencapsulated devices which were exposed to continuous illumination conditions with an LED lamp $400{-}800~\mathrm{nm}$ , power density equivalent to $100\mathrm{~mW~cm}^{-2}$ calibrated by standard silicon cells). The device with CL-MCz maintained $91\%$ of the initial efficiency after $560\mathrm{~h~}$ illumination, whereas only $70\%$ of the initial value was attained in the PTAA-based devices under the same condition (Figure 6A). In addition, we studied the thermal stability of devices stored in an inert environment under continuous heating at $65~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . The devices employing CL-MCz as HTM presented enhanced tolerance to thermal stress by maintaining $88\%$ of initial PCE after aging for $335\mathrm{~h~}$ , while the PTAA-based device only remained $44\%$ of initial efficiency after aging for $288\mathrm{~h~}$ (Figure 6B). The improvement of thermal and photostability of CL-MCz-based devices could be mostly attributed to the improvement of perovskite quality and strong interfacial interaction between CL-MCz and the perovskite. In addition, the improvement of thermal stability could be attributed to the increase of $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ of CL-MCz film. Finally, we evaluated the long-term stability of the unencapsulated devices which were stored in $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ atmosphere. Specifically, only $6\%$ reduction of the initial PCEs was found in CLMCz-based devices after $2500\mathrm{~h~}$ , whereas the devices based on PTAA and CL-VNPB lost their initial efficiency by $42\%$ and $24\%$ under the same condition (Figure 6C).
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, we have designed and synthesized a low-cost, multifunctional small molecule MCz-VPOZ, which can be spontaneously crosslinked by thermal annealing for fabricating a polymer hole conductor for inverted PSCs. The resulting polymer CL-MCz films show remarkable chemical resistance, high chargeconducting capacity, and significant defect passivation effects. In addition, high energy ordering and appropriate HOMO level of CL-MCz significantly result in reduced charge-transport barrier as well as an extremely small energy loss (0.41 eV). Besides, CL-MCz demonstrates superior surface wettability, which can facilitate the formation of high-quality perovskite film with high crystallinity as well as reduced defects at the buried interfaces. By employing such a conductive and hydrophilic CL-MCz as HTL, the corresponding devices yield a champion PCE of $23.9\%$ with simultaneously enhanced photovoltaic parameters, which represents the highest reported efficiency for non-PTAA polymer HTMs. Moreover, the unencapsulated devices with CL-MCz- exhibit impressive shelf-life stability in various environmental conditions up to $2500\mathrm{~h~}$ . This work provides an alternative molecular strategy for designing HTLs toward efficient and stable inverted PSCs, which will pave the way to further development of PSC technology.
## 4. Experimental Section
Lead Contact: Further information and requests for resources and materials should be directed to and will be full filled by the Lead Contact, Jinbao Zhang (jinbao.zhang $@$ xmu.edu.cn).
Materials Availability: MCz-VPOZ generated in this study will be made available on reasonable request, but we may require payment and/ or a completed Materials Transfer Agreement if there is potential for commercial application.
Data and Code Availability: This study did not generate any datasets or codes. The data and results supporting the current study are available from the Lead Contact upon reasonable request.
Materials: All the commercial materials were used as received, including N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF, $99.99\%$ , dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO, $99.9\%$ , isopropanol (IPA, $99.5\%$ ) chlorobenzene (CB, $99.9\%$ , Bisphenol A diglycidyl ether (D3415), which were purchased from SigmaAldrich. Lead iodide $(\mathsf{P b l}_{2})$ , lead chloride $({\mathsf{P b C l}}_{2})$ , methylammonium iodide (MAI), formamidinium iodide (FAI), poly (triaryl amine) (PTAA), N4, ${\sf N}4^{\prime}$ -di(naphthalen-1-yl)-N4, ${\mathsf{N}}4^{\prime}$ -bis(4-vinylphenyl) biphenyl$^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -diamine (VNPB) and fullerene $(C_{60})$ were purchased from Xi’an Polymer Light Technology Corp. (China). $^{2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}}$ -Tetrakis [N, N-di(4- methoxyphenyl) amino]- $^{9,9^{\prime}}$ -spiro-bifluorene (spiro-OMeTAD), Indium tin oxide glass (ITO) and Fluorine-doped tin oxide glass (FTO) glass substrate were purchased from Advanced Election Technology CO., Ltd (China).
Preparation of Precursors: The perovskite precursors were mixed stoichiometric (1.17 m) $\mathsf{P b l}_{2},$ $(0.73~\mathsf{M})$ $\mathsf{P b C l}_{2}$ , $(0.2~\mathsf{m})$ FAI, and (1.1 m) MAI in $1m L$ DMF/DMSO $(\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v}=9/1)$ ) by stirring for $2\ h$ at $60~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and then aged at $25^{\circ}C$ for $8\ h$ . PTAA and VNPB precursor was $2~\mathsf{m g}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1}$ dissolved in CB. The $_{\mathsf{M C z}}$ -VPOZ with different concentrations (0.5, 1, 1.5 $,2m g\mathsf{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1})$ ) were dissolved in CB. BCP was dissolved in ethanol at a concentration of $0.5~\mathsf{m g}~\mathsf{m l}^{-1}$ .
Device Fabrication: ITO glass was cleaned by sequentially washing with detergent, deionized water, acetone, and isopropanol (IPA) for $20\mathrm{\min}$ , respectively. The ITO was dried with the dry air and treated with UV ozone for 15 min before use. PTAA, VNPB, and MCz-VPOZ were spin-coated onto the cleaned ITO at 4000  rpm for $30\mathrm{~s~}$ followed by annealing at ${\mathsf{100}}\ {\mathsf{^{o}C}}$ for $10\mathrm{\min}$ . Before the spin-coating process, the $(\mathsf{F A}_{0.17}\mathsf{M A}_{0.94}\mathsf{P b}_{13.17})_{0.95}(\mathsf{P b}\mathsf{C l}_{2})_{0.05}$ perovskite precursor solution was filtered with a Teflon filter $(0.45~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ ). Perovskite films were prepared by spin-coating at 1200 rpm for $3\ \mathsf{s}$ , 5000 rpm for $28~\mathsf{s}$ , and $150~\upmu\uptau\uptau$ was dropped on the substrate $8\:s$ prior to the end of the second stage. Then, the substrate was annealed at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 1 min, $\mathsf{l}00^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $10\min$ . A $30~\mathsf{n m}$ of ${\mathsf{C}}_{60}$ layer was deposited on the perovskite layer by thermal evaporation at a high vacuum $(\approx3\times70^{-5}~\mathsf{P a})$ ). BCP was deposited as cathode buffer layers at a rate of 4000 rpm. Finally, the $\mathsf{A g}$ back electrode $(\approx]20~\mathsf{n m})$ was deposited by thermal evaporation at a high vacuum $(\approx3\times10^{-5}\mathsf{P a})$ .
The Lift-Off Process: The exfoliation process of the perovskite film for investigating the buried interface was described in our previous work.[46] Specifically, the HTL and perovskite layer were deposited on the flexible ITO in sequence by spin coating. A layer of PMMA was deposited on the perovskite surface via blade-coating. Then, the epoxy was obtained via mixing the Bisphenol A diglycidyl ether, Octylamine, and m-xylylenediamine with a molar ratio of 4:2:1, by stirring the solution for $10\min$ at $70~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . The epoxy was deposited on the PMMA layer by blade-coating and solidified for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . Finally, the perovskite film could be peeled off by bending the flexible device with a structure of ITO/HTL/ perovskite/PMMA/epoxy.
Density Functional Theory: According to the chemical environment of experimental synthesis and previous theoretical results,[54] the $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ -terminated (001) surface of the room-temperature tetragonal phase (space group I4cm) of $\mathsf{M A P b l}_{3}$ as the substrate was chosen. In this paper, a recently-proposed BOSS method[38] was applied, to accelerate the computation of 4D energy landscapes. As an activelearning method, BOSS fitted a surrogate model to existing data using the Gaussian process, then acquit further data with a smart-sampling strategy based on this surrogate model until convergence Practically, BOSS was employed to efficiently sample single-point DFT calculations for PC- or PPO perovskite structures defined by different $(\mathsf{x},\mathsf{y},\mathsf{z},\theta)$ . DFT calculations were performed using the Perdew–Burke–Ernzerhof exchange-correlation functional,[55] augmented with long-range van der Waals corrections based on the Tkatchenko-Scheffler method,[56] implemented in the all-electron numeric atom-centered orbital code FHIaims.[57,58] The scalar relativistic effects using the zeroth-order regular approximation[59] were also included. Previous studies had confirmed the validity of these computational settings to properly describe hybrid perovskite systems.[60,61] Within the BOSS scheme, the total-energy landscapes of all complexes were converged with 400–500 DFT singlepoint calculations, which was much faster than the conventional equispaced grid-point approach for a 4D problem.
Characterization and Measurements: The $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR and $^{13}{\mathsf C}$ NMR spectra were recorded on the Bruker Ascend $400~\mathsf{M H z}$ spectrometer. High-resolution mass spectra were obtained with ThermoScientificTM $\textsf{Q}$ -Exactive. Elemental analyses of compounds were performed using a elemental analyzer with model EA3000 at Shenzhen University (Shenzhen, Guangdong, China).
The microscopic film surface and cross-section SEM images were obtained with a field-emission SEM (S-4800, Hitachi).  XRD spectra were measured by Ultima IV of Rigaku with $\mathsf{C u}\ \mathsf{K}\alpha$ radiation (1.5406 Å). The water contact angles were measured by KRUSS GmbH, DSA20. The photographs were taken 1s after the water dripped. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was conducted on a Mettler, STARe TA Instrument at a heating ramp of $70\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\ \mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ under an ${\sf N}_{2}$ flow rate of $90\ m L\ m i n^{-1}$ . UV–vis absorption spectra were recorded on a Shimadzu UV-3600 UV–VIS–NIR spectrophotometer. PL spectra were obtained by FLS1000 (Edinburgh instruments) with an excitation at $460\ n m$ , and TRPL spectra were obtained by FLS1000 (Edinburgh instruments) with an excitation at $450\ \mathsf{n m}$ . AFM spectra were measured by Dimension Icon (Bruker AXS) at tapping mode. KPFM was measured by Cypher ES (Oxford Instruments Asylum Research) via AC mode at $2.44~\mathsf{H z}$ with ASYELEC.01-R2 probe. EIS measurements were measured by using an electrochemical workstation (CHI 660) with a frequency range from 10 to $800~\mathsf{k H z}$ at an open circuit voltage in dark conditions. Mott-Schottky analysis was conducted by using an electrochemical workstation (CHI 660) at a frequency of $7k H z$ with potential ranging from 0 to $\mathsf{l}.2\mathsf{V}.$
For PSC device characterization, all $J{-}V$ characteristics of the devices were measured under the simulated AM1.5G irradiation $(700~\mathsf{m w}\mathsf{c m}^{-2})$ using a Xe lamp-based SS-F5-3A Solar Simulator (Enli Technology, Inc.). A Xe lamp equipped with an AM1.5G filter was used as the white light source. The light intensity was controlled with an NREL-calibrated Si solar cell with a KG-5 filter. The devices were measured both in reverse scan ( $1.2\:\forall\to-0.2\:\forall,$ step 0.02 V) and forward scan $(-0.2\lor\rightarrow\lor.2\lor,$ step 0.02  V) with $2m s$ delay time. The EQE was measured by a QE-R3011 measurement system (Enli Technology, Inc.).
